<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jetavana</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jetavana was a monastery in a grove outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravasti">Shravasti</a>, the capital of Kosala, given to the Buddha by the merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anathapindika">Anathapindika</a>. The Buddha is said to have spent 19 rainy seasons there, more than anywhere else, and many of his discourses are set in it. Its remains, excavated from the 19th century, form part of the site of Sahet-Mahet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jetavana.jpg" alt="Jetavana" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: myself · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mulagandhakuti.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
